\title[Stack polyominoes with a square core]{Stack Polyominoes with a Square Core:\\
Structure, Regularity, and Stretched-Exponential Growth}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{26 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
A \emph{stack polyomino} is a bottom-justified, column-convex polyomino whose column-height
profile is unimodal. Its \emph{core} is the maximal plateau; the stack has a \emph{square core}
when that plateau consists of exactly $k$ columns of common height $k$, so the crown of the
shape is a $k\times k$ square. Let $a(n)$ be the number of square-core stacks of area $n$.

Slicing each stack as (left slope) $\Vert$ ($k\times k$ core) $\Vert$ (right slope) yields the
exact layer decomposition
\[
  a(n)=\sum_{k^{2}\le n}\ \sum_{i+j=n-k^{2}}p_{\le k-1}(i)\,p_{\le k-1}(j),
  \qquad
  \sum_{n\ge0}a(n)x^{n}=\sum_{k\ge0}\frac{x^{k^{2}}}{\prod_{i=1}^{k-1}(1-x^{i})^{2}},
\]
where $p_{\le b}$ counts partitions into parts of size at most $b$; the slicing is a bijection
onto genuine unimodal column-height lists. We deduce: the vanishing set
$\{n:a(n)=0\}=\{2,3\}$; the bound $a(n)\ge n-3$ and strict monotonicity from $n=4$;
convexity $2a(n+1)\le a(n)+a(n+2)$ for $n\ge2$, from an exact difference identity for
self-convolutions; divergence of the increments; and two sharp negative results, the failure of
log-concavity ($a(8)^{2}=25<28=a(7)a(9)$) and of third-order convexity
($a(10)+3a(8)=24<25=3a(9)+a(7)$). The latter is explained by an exact closed form for the third
core layer $c_{2}$: $24\,c_{2}(2s)=(2s+2)(2s+3)(2s+4)$ and $24\,c_{2}(2s+1)=(2s+2)(2s+4)(2s+6)$,
whence $\Delta^{3}c_{2}(2t)=-(t+2)$ and $\Delta^{3}c_{2}(2t+1)=t+3$ --- an infinite family of
violations of linearly growing amplitude. Finally, an elementary Rankin-type bound gives
$\log p_{\le b}(m)\le 8\sqrt m+12$ \emph{uniformly in $b$}, and together with an exponential
lower bound from a single core layer yields
\[
  \tfrac12(\sqrt n-2)\log2\ \le\ \log a(n)\ \le\ 30\sqrt n \qquad (n\ge100),
\]
so $\log a(n)\asymp\sqrt n$. Hence $a$ is superpolynomial yet subexponential and the entropy
density obeys $\log a(n)/n\to0$: as an interface model, square-core stacks carry no extensive
entropy. Numerics indicate the true constant is the Hardy--Ramanujan constant $\pi\sqrt{2/3}$,
and we explain the responsible saddle-point mechanism.
\end{abstract}

