\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The objects}
A \emph{polyomino} is a finite edge-connected set of unit cells in $\Z^{2}$. A bottom-justified,
column-convex polyomino is determined by its list of column heights
$L=(h_{1},\dots,h_{r})$ with $h_{i}\ge1$. A \emph{stack polyomino} is such a shape whose profile
is \emph{unimodal}: $L=P\Vert S$ with $P$ weakly increasing and $S$ weakly decreasing. The
\emph{area} is $|L|=h_{1}+\dots+h_{r}$.

Let $k=\max_{i}h_{i}$. The \emph{core} is the maximal plateau, i.e.\ the (by unimodality
consecutive) columns of height exactly $k$. The stack has a \emph{square core} when
$\#\{i: h_{i}=k\}=k$.

\begin{definition}\label{def:a}
$a(n)$ is the number of lists $L$ with $|L|=n$, all entries $\ge1$, $L$ unimodal, and
$\#\{i:h_{i}=\max L\}=\max L$. The empty list is included, so $a(0)=1$.
\end{definition}

The first thirty-two values are
\[
1,1,0,0,1,2,3,4,5,7,9,13,17,24,31,42,54,71,90,117,147,188,236,298,371,466,576,716,882,1088,1331,1633.
\]

\subsection{Motivation}
Combinatorially, the square-core condition is a \emph{ceiling} on the parts of two slope
partitions, and the resulting generating function is a one-factor truncation of the classical
Durfee-square series for the partition function. Physically, a stack polyomino rotated by ninety
degrees is a discrete solid-on-solid interface with a single hump; the area is the particle
number and $\log a(n)$ the microcanonical entropy, so the growth order of $\log a(n)$ decides
whether the model has extensive entropy. Analytically, the answer $\log a(n)\asymp\sqrt n$ places
the family in the stretched-exponential regime and raises the question of the exact constant.

